WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:08.363
MCP A Creator and the One Hub: our entry in the GSA MCP Server and SI Agent Government Hackathon.

2
00:00:08.543 --> 00:00:15.014
It is one solution with four layers, proven on one ship, the aircraft carrier CVN-79.

3
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.041
Here is the problem.

4
00:00:17.221 --> 00:00:23.947
The Department of Defense plans to spend more than 2.4 trillion dollars on its costliest weapon programs,

5
00:00:23.947 --> 00:00:27.718
and it has gone nine years without a clean audit opinion.

6
00:00:27.898 --> 00:00:34.142
No office can trace what a weapon system costs, from the budget request, through the contracts, to the asset.

7
00:00:34.322 --> 00:00:46.766
CVN-79 shows it. The ship has four official costs, from 11,341 million dollars to 13,409.97 million.

8
00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:50.746
None of them agree, and 59 questions stay open underneath.

9
00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:53.627
We built one solution in four layers.

10
00:00:53.807 --> 00:01:00.598
Layer one, MCP A Creator, builds a tested, governed MCP server from a question and a table.

11
00:01:00.778 --> 00:01:06.251
Layer two is the domain servers. They give agents cited access to budget documents,

12
00:01:06.251 --> 00:01:09.008
contract records and the work registers.

13
00:01:09.188 --> 00:01:16.279
Layer three is the agent orchestration environment, where four specialist agents propose and people decide.

14
00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:21.382
Layer four is the financial-control layer. It analyzes in exact decimals,

15
00:01:21.382 --> 00:01:28.650
reconciles every figure to its source, records where each number came from, and decides who has authority.

16
00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:33.022
No model sits in that layer. That is what sets this work apart:

17
00:01:33.022 --> 00:01:37.601
AI-assisted development, joined to deterministic financial control.

18
00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:40.092
The entry covers three tracks.

19
00:01:40.272 --> 00:01:47.292
For dataset access, the docs, analyst and ops servers query open budget, oversight and contract data.

20
00:01:47.472 --> 00:01:52.298
For service read, the One Hub server and the Ask page retrieve the state of the work.

21
00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:59.963
For service write, a person's decision is submitted under their own identity, in a sandbox workspace over public data.

22
00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:05.343
All of it runs on Databricks today, as seven apps: the docs, analyst,

23
00:02:05.343 --> 00:02:10.325
ops and One Hub servers, MCP A Creator, the probe, and the front center.

24
00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:14.824
Each one deploys from the public GitHub repository with one command.

25
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.852
Six MCP servers expose 63 tools.

26
00:02:19.032 --> 00:02:28.101
The docs server reads 34 PDFs, 7,804 pages, down to the page and the table, with no model in the loop.

27
00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:32.962
The analyst server works a record that is missing data, and names what to ask for.

28
00:02:33.142 --> 00:02:39.808
The ops server serves exhibit cells, obligations and the registers. MCP A Creator built it.

29
00:02:39.988 --> 00:02:42.856
The One Hub server holds the gate and the gap queue.

30
00:02:43.036 --> 00:02:49.099
The bakehouse server, also built by the Creator, reads a sales table that has nothing to do with ships.

31
00:02:49.279 --> 00:02:55.402
Every input is validated, and 421 hostile calls against the read tools wrote nothing.

32
00:02:56.117 --> 00:02:58.992
MCP A Creator is the part that scales.

33
00:02:59.172 --> 00:03:03.527
Give it a question and a Unity Catalog table. It profiles the table,

34
00:03:03.527 --> 00:03:09.411
designs one tool per question, reviews each tool, and generates a full project with tests.

35
00:03:09.591 --> 00:03:15.539
It deploys that project as the requester's own app, after an approval bound to the exact files.

36
00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:19.401
Skills carry the judgment, and generators write the code,

37
00:03:19.401 --> 00:03:26.951
so one specification gives the same project every time. Fifteen of fifteen generated projects pass their own tests.

38
00:03:27.131 --> 00:03:31.632
We also measured it where it was weakest. On tables it had never seen,

39
00:03:31.632 --> 00:03:34.902
its word rules read 10 of 19 questions correctly.

40
00:03:35.082 --> 00:03:40.486
So each tool now shows how it read the question. The calling model corrects it in one line,

41
00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:43.516
and code checks the correction against the table.

42
00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.075
The fourth layer is why the numbers can be trusted.

43
00:03:47.255 --> 00:03:53.539
Code reads the records and adds them in exact decimals. Each figure is tied back to its source,

44
00:03:53.539 --> 00:03:56.835
and carries its document, its page and its cell.

45
00:03:57.015 --> 00:04:02.953
A gate with no model in it decides: promote, send to review, log a gap, or hold as a lead.

46
00:04:03.132 --> 00:04:06.601
And only the office that owns a record can approve a change.

47
00:04:06.781 --> 00:04:12.639
A model may plan, explain and draft. It can never add a figure, change an amount, or approve.

48
00:04:13.383 --> 00:04:16.149
The front center is where people use all of this.

49
00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:20.302
It opens on the solution itself: the four layers, the three tracks,

50
00:04:20.302 --> 00:04:25.048
and the five steps that prove a figure, each linked to the page that shows it.

51
00:04:25.583 --> 00:04:31.150
Below that are the seven capabilities. For each one, the page shows the problem it solves,

52
00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.609
how it was tested, and what each result does not show.

53
00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:42.748
Impact answers the bigger question. You ask. Agents plan. MCP servers fetch. The gate checks. You decide.

54
00:04:42.928 --> 00:04:47.219
A new program supplies data, not software. It brings its documents,

55
00:04:47.219 --> 00:04:50.990
its contract numbers and a role map, and reuses the rest.

56
00:04:51.717 --> 00:04:57.175
Position answers the first question a leader asks: which figure do I quote, and for what?

57
00:04:57.355 --> 00:05:02.846
The page compares every pair, and walks the bridge from the cap to the budget, step by step.

58
00:05:03.583 --> 00:05:12.750
Cost composition breaks the 13,409.97 million dollar total down by cost category and by funding stream.

59
00:05:13.483 --> 00:05:19.740
Budget execution shows the obligation curve, the handoff between contracts, and the largest actions.

60
00:05:20.483 --> 00:05:24.184
Here is the agentic workflow, live, on the deployed app.

61
00:05:24.364 --> 00:05:30.553
From Position, we ask a specialist to explain an account period that does not tie to the transactions.

62
00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:33.896
The orchestrator routes it to the Account Analyst.

63
00:05:34.076 --> 00:05:37.351
The steps stream in, each one labeled code or model.

64
00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:43.822
The account file exceeds the closest transaction window by 770 million dollars.

65
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:51.928
The run proposes two things: a lead for the Office of Financial Operations, and a request for the obligation detail.

66
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.954
Now the access control. The signed-in user holds the program office role.

67
00:05:57.134 --> 00:06:04.288
The first proposal belongs to Financial Operations, so the app says so, and refuses to let this user apply it.

68
00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:09.035
The second proposal is owned by the program office. The user applies it,

69
00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:12.712
and the action is recorded with the role and the run number.

70
00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.004
Every run follows the same six stages.

71
00:06:16.184 --> 00:06:23.088
A page triggers one task on one subject. Code packs the records, with every figure in exact decimals.

72
00:06:23.268 --> 00:06:28.947
One model explains. It cannot add a figure, change an amount, or change who owns an item.

73
00:06:29.127 --> 00:06:34.132
Code then grounds every number, and a model from a second family reads the answer.

74
00:06:34.312 --> 00:06:41.573
Last, only the role that owns an item can apply it. Unity Catalog makes that call under the user's own token.

75
00:06:42.317 --> 00:06:47.225
The gap register asks which open questions block the number, and who can close them.

76
00:06:47.405 --> 00:06:51.690
It splits the work into what can be done now, and what needs an access request.

77
00:06:52.417 --> 00:06:58.523
From any gap, an agent drafts the request. Here the Gap Investigator works gap G52.

78
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:02.247
It proposes collection requests addressed to the offices

79
00:07:02.247 --> 00:07:06.136
that hold the records, and the checker re-checks every condition.

80
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.078
Nothing is saved until a person with the right role applies it.

81
00:07:10.817 --> 00:07:15.269
Ask the evidence takes a plain question, and answers it from the same records.

82
00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:21.042
What was obligated on the contract in fiscal 2022, and what was the largest action?

83
00:07:21.533 --> 00:07:27.527
The wait is cut. The answer comes back as a lead, with its citation and the records behind it.

84
00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:31.154
On the deployed app, Ask is an MCP host.

85
00:07:31.334 --> 00:07:37.266
It lists the tools of the docs, ops and One Hub servers, and each step names the server it used.

86
00:07:37.446 --> 00:07:42.125
Asked whether the ship can be briefed at 13,529 million dollars,

87
00:07:42.125 --> 00:07:47.070
it finds the budget line, rebuilds the exhibit, and runs the gate.

88
00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:52.318
The gate calls the result a lead to verify. It does not call it a reportable figure.

89
00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:58.623
A second question: which account periods fail to tie to the transactions, and by how much?

90
00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:03.232
The answer lists each period and its difference, with the SQL that ran.

91
00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.729
The work queue shows what needs a decision, what is blocked, and why.

92
00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:13.807
Each run keeps its full record: the steps, the checks code ran again, and the checker's verdict.

93
00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:23.235
Brief readiness shows what the brief can stand on. Live, 13 of 32 CVN-79 figures tie to source records.

94
00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:32.714
The Obligation Reconciler adds 927 contract actions in exact decimals, and code re-checks eight of eight conditions.

95
00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:37.893
Its finding goes to the program office as a proposal, for a person to decide.

96
00:08:38.617 --> 00:08:42.135
Method explains how this is built, and why it can be challenged.

97
00:08:42.315 --> 00:08:48.528
It keeps three trust zones with a one-way door, and it follows one rule. No model promotes a figure.

98
00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:56.111
Finally, the people who know. Answer a question opens the 59 open questions to experts at headquarters

99
00:08:56.111 --> 00:08:57.374
and in the field.

100
00:08:57.554 --> 00:09:01.471
Each question names who is likely to know: the program office,

101
00:09:01.471 --> 00:09:06.392
financial operations, the supervisor of shipbuilding, or the finance center.

102
00:09:06.572 --> 00:09:10.252
An expert writes what they know, how they know it, and how sure they are.

103
00:09:10.432 --> 00:09:15.174
The answer is recorded with their name as a lead, routed to the gap it answers,

104
00:09:15.174 --> 00:09:18.895
and never treated as a certified figure until it is checked.

105
00:09:19.617 --> 00:09:20.930
How do we know it works?

106
00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:26.063
On 69 sealed questions, hashed before any model saw them,

107
00:09:26.063 --> 00:09:33.656
Claude Sonnet 4.5 answers 88.4 percent with the servers, against 14.5 percent without.

108
00:09:33.836 --> 00:09:39.253
GPT-5 mini goes from 1.4 percent to 78.7.

109
00:09:39.433 --> 00:09:44.805
On questions written by an evaluator outside the team, Claude scores 25 of 30.

110
00:09:44.985 --> 00:09:51.974
A separate outside reviewer, working blind, would rely on 9 of 10 answers with the servers, and none without.

111
00:09:52.154 --> 00:09:59.034
1,770 tests pass from a clean clone, and 828 of them are hostile inputs.

112
00:09:59.214 --> 00:10:13.596
On the deployed store, 7,743 of 7,746 budget tie-outs pass. And the probe passes 99.9 percent of 6,193 live checks.

113
00:10:14.317 --> 00:10:15.581
It is built to run.

114
00:10:15.761 --> 00:10:20.913
Every app requires workspace sign-in. Decisions run under the user's own token.

115
00:10:21.093 --> 00:10:27.175
Tool inputs are validated and capped, identifiers are allow-listed, and parameters are bound.

116
00:10:27.355 --> 00:10:30.847
And the probe calls every deployed server every 15 minutes.

117
00:10:31.583 --> 00:10:34.295
The next program supplies data, not software.

118
00:10:34.475 --> 00:10:40.359
Inside the Department, the path runs from CVN-79 to the Ford class,

119
00:10:40.359 --> 00:10:46.715
then 29 Navy ship lines, the F-35, and all 59 programs on one template.

120
00:10:46.895 --> 00:10:52.366
Beyond defense, every agency has the same shape of problem: budget justifications,

121
00:10:52.366 --> 00:10:56.957
contract and grant records, and audit findings it cannot yet close.

122
00:10:57.683 --> 00:11:02.677
AI builds and explains. Code proves the number. The owning role decides.

123
00:11:02.857 --> 00:11:06.780
One ship, reconciled to the cent, and ready for the next program.

